<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enryaku-ji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/788">788</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enryaku-ji is a Buddhist monastery on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, founded by the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saicho">Saichō</a> in 788 and the head temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> school. Over the following centuries it grew into a vast complex with its own armies of warrior monks, who often intervened in the politics of the capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> burned it and massacred its inhabitants in 1571; it was later rebuilt and is part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enryaku-ji.jpg" alt="Enryaku-ji" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enryakuji_Konponchudo02s5s3200.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Enryaku-ji is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> monastery on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a> in Ōtsu, overlooking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. It was founded in 788, in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saicho">Saichō</a>, also known as Dengyō Daishi, who introduced the Tendai sect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. With the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-kanmu">Emperor Kanmu</a>, Saichō ordained a hundred disciples in 807. He kept strict discipline on the mountain, and his monks lived in seclusion for twelve years of study and meditation. The best students were then kept on in the monastery, and others went into government posts. The founders of Jōdo-shū, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">Jōdo Shinshū</a>, Sōtō Zen and Nichiren Buddhism all spent time at the monastery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At its peak Enryaku-ji was a huge complex of as many as 3,000 sub-temples with a powerful army of warrior monks, the sōhei. In the tenth century succession disputes broke out between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> monks of the line of Ennin and those of Enchin. They produced rival centres at Enryaku-ji and at Mii-dera, known as the Mountain Order and the Temple Order. Warrior monks were used to settle the disputes, and Tendai leaders hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> armies that threatened rivals and even marched on the capital to enforce monastic demands.</p><p>The warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> ended this militancy in 1571, as part of a programme to remove all potential rivals and unite the country. He attacked Enryaku-ji, levelled its buildings and killed monks. The present structures date from the late sixteenth century to the first half of the seventeenth, when the temple was rebuilt after a change of government. The most significant reconstruction, that of the main hall, took place in 1642 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-iemitsu">Tokugawa Iemitsu</a>. Only one minor building survived, the thirteenth-century Ruri-dō, or Lapis Lazuli Hall. Some buildings were moved from other temples, notably Mii-dera.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Most of the buildings stand in three areas: Tō-dō, the East Pagoda, which holds the most important buildings, Sai-tō, the West Pagoda, and the more isolated Yokokawa. Enryaku-ji is the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> sect and part of the UNESCO World Heritage Site Historic Monuments of Ancient Kyoto. It is also the centre for the practice of kaihōgyō, undertaken by the so-called marathon monks.</p><p>On 4 April 2006 Enryaku-ji performed a ceremony for former leaders of the Yamaguchi-gumi, the largest yakuza organisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and received crime-related money for it, despite a request from the Shiga Prefectural Police to stop. After newspaper reports the temple faced a nationwide scandal, and on 18 May all its representative directors resigned.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Enryaku-ji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He burned the <b>Enryaku-ji</b> on Mount Hiei in 1571, defeated the Takeda at Nagashino in 1575 with massed muskets and fought the Ishiyama Hongan-ji until 1580.</blockquote><a href="/state/oda-regime">Oda regime</a></li></ul></section>
